>
  <p>Chords are the fastest route from “I can’t play” to “I can play that song”. Most popular music is a melody sitting on top of a few repeating chords. Learn the chords and a rhythm to play them in, and you can accompany yourself or a singer long before you can read a page of music.</p>

  <h2 id="what">What a chord is</h2>
  <p>A chord is three or more notes played together. The simplest kind, a <b>triad</b>, has three: a root, a third and a fifth. The root gives the chord its name. C major starts on C.</p>
  <p>You can build any triad by counting semitones, the distance from one key to the very next key, black or white:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Major:</b> root, up 4 semitones, up 3 more. C → E → G.</li>
    <li><b>Minor:</b> root, up 3 semitones, up 4 more. A → C → E.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Major chords sound bright and settled. Minor chords sound softer and sadder. Play C major, then C minor (C–E♭–G), and you’ll hear the difference straight away.</p>

  <h2 id="major-minor">Major and minor shapes</h2>
  <p>On the white keys, a root-position triad always looks the same: play a key, skip one, play one, skip one, play one. Your hand makes the shape, and you slide it along to change chord. C, F and G major all use this shape on white keys only, and so do D minor, E minor and A minor.</p>
  <p>That’s why the piano is friendly for beginners. On guitar, every chord has its own fingering. On the piano, you learn one shape and move it.</p>

  <h2 id="try">Try it on the keyboard</h2>
  <p>Tap a chord name to see which keys to press and hear how it sounds. The blue key is the bass note for your left hand. The gold keys are your right hand.</p>
  <ChordExplorer id="chord-guide" />

  <h2 id="key-of-c">The chords in C major</h2>
  <p>Every major key has six chords you’ll use all the time, plus one you’ll rarely need. In C major they’re all on white keys:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Chord</th><th scope="col">Notes</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Roman numeral</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">C</th><td>C E G</td><td>Major</td><td>I</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Dm</th><td>D F A</td><td>Minor</td><td>ii</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Em</th><td>E G B</td><td>Minor</td><td>iii</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">F</th><td>F A C</td><td>Major</td><td>IV</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">G</th><td>G B D</td><td>Major</td><td>V</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Am</th><td>A C E</td><td>Minor</td><td>vi</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">B°</th><td>B D F</td><td>Diminished</td><td>vii°</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The Roman numerals let musicians talk about progressions in any key. “I–V–vi–IV” means C–G–Am–F in C major, and G–D–Em–C in G major. Same pattern, different starting point.</p>
  <p>Once C major feels comfortable, learn the same chords in G major (G, Am, Bm, C, D, Em) and F major (F, Gm, Am, B♭, C, Dm). Those three keys cover a large share of popular songs.</p>

  <h2 id="hands">Fingering and both hands</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Right hand:</b> thumb, middle finger and little finger (1–3–5) on the three notes.</li>
    <li><b>Left hand:</b> start with just the root note, played with your little finger or thumb. Later, play the full chord with 5–3–1, or the root and fifth together.</li>
    <li><b>Posture:</b> fingers curved as if holding a ball, wrists level, shoulders down. If anything tightens, stop and shake it out.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Playing the root in the left hand while the right hand holds the chord gives you a full, two-handed sound from the first day. Add a rhythm in the right hand, and it already sounds like accompaniment.</p>

  <h2 id="progressions">Your first progressions</h2>
  <p>Practise changing between chords slowly and evenly, four beats on each. These progressions turn up again and again:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Progression</th><th scope="col">In C major</th><th scope="col">Where you’ll hear it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">I–V–vi–IV</th><td>C–G–Am–F</td><td>Countless modern pop songs</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">I–vi–IV–V</th><td>C–Am–F–G</td><td>1950s doo-wop and ballads</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">I–IV–V</th><td>C–F–G</td><td>Folk, rock ’n’ roll, country</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">12-bar blues</th><td>C C C C · F F C C · G F C G</td><td>Blues and early rock ’n’ roll</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>When the changes feel smooth, try a rhythm: hold the bass note in the left hand and play the right-hand chord on every beat, or break it up into single notes (a broken chord). Pianoforall’s first book teaches fifteen of these rhythm styles.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Inversions and seventh chords</h2>
  <p>Playing every chord in root position makes your hand jump. <b>Inversions</b> fix that. C major can be C–E–G, E–G–C or G–C–E. Moving from C (C–E–G) to F, you can play F as C–F–A: your thumb stays on C and only two fingers move.</p>
  <p><b>Seventh chords</b> add a fourth note and a richer sound. G7 is G–B–D–F, and it pulls strongly back to C. Cmaj7 (C–E–G–B) and Am7 (A–C–E–G) give ballads and jazz their soft colour. Try them in the keyboard above.</p>
  <p>Pianoforall covers inversions in Book 3 and richer chords in Book 4, after the rhythm basics are in your hands.</p>
</Article>
